From ab2ed9a20dcb6a1cb5d0a758d2a1322964e0f46f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:56:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Manager: every purchased CPU share is confined by its exact
 guest quota, below one processor too

Hyper-V's root scheduler (the NucBox's Windows 11 client host, scheduler type 0x4) does not enforce
a VM's processor Limit/Reservation: root's sustained native run burned 4000M in 4813 ms at 100% of
one VP and 4824 ms at a read-back 16%. The guest app cgroup quota (wmiserve --app-cpu, monitor
allocatedPolicy) is the only cap, and the launcher sent it only above the catalog's cpuPercent 100,
so every share up to 6.25% of 16 processors (1%, 2%, 4%...) ran uncapped within its whole VP.

cpuForShare now states appCpuPercent = floor(entitlement / 1000), the purchased share in percent of
one processor (1% of 16 -> 16, 2% -> 32, 4% -> 64, 13% -> 208, 21% -> 336, 25% -> 400; exact for
whole-percent shares, rounded down otherwise, never up past what was bought). With
ENCLAVE_GUEST_CPU_ALLOWANCE=1 the launcher sends it for EVERY share, and wmiserve-run's strict echo
(unchanged) refuses to serve, removing the VM, unless the monitor applied exactly it.
The record no longer states a realizable capacity built from the catalog cap or the (unenforced) WMI
read-back: enforcement is 'guest-app-cgroup' with enforcedUnits only on the exact echo, otherwise
'none' with the reason. VP sizing, the WMI processor step and its read-back check, pool admission,
RAM, the readiness witness and the GPU image's settings are unchanged; the node's catalog GFLOPS floor
(effectiveCpuShare) still decides the share.
Tests: 1/2/4/13/21/25% quotas through the real launcher chain, missing / catalog-100 / pre-fix-guest
refusals, the node's floor carried by the quota, no read-back claims.
